        new("Stereo & upmix", "LFE crossover",
            "The cut-off frequency used to create the subwoofer (LFE) channel from stereo bass.",
            "This only builds the LFE signal; your AVR's own bass management still decides what the subwoofer plays. Keep it near your AVR's crossover. LFE generation uses the same crossover. Without a dedicated subwoofer nothing creates an LFE, so the slider is greyed out (its value is kept).",
            "80 Hz (THX standard).",
            "lfe crossover subwoofer bass 80hz"),
        new("Stereo & upmix", "LFE level",
            "Level of the generated subwoofer channel (stereo upmix and LFE generation).",
            "Dolby/DTS decoders play LFE 10 dB louder than the other channels, so -10 dB here means \"same loudness as the bass in the original\". Without a dedicated subwoofer no LFE is created at all (the fronts already play that bass), so the slider is greyed out; the level of a source's own LFE folded into the fronts is set with the LFE trim on the Mixer.",
            "-10 dB. Raise it for more bass.",
            "lfe level subwoofer bass boost"),
        new("Stereo & upmix", "LFE generation (5.0 \u2192 5.1)",
            "One switch with a meaning that depends on the \"Dedicated subwoofer\" switch.\n\nWith a dedicated subwoofer it controls LFE generation for sources that have no LFE of their own. Some games and films are mixed as 5.0 (or 7.0): surround, but no subwoofer channel. LFE generation builds one from the bass of all channels, using the LFE crossover and LFE level above. It is separate from the stereo upmixer.\n• Auto: only when the source has played surround for 3 s with a silent LFE; fades in over 0.5 s and out over 0.3 s when the source brings its own LFE.\n• Always: immediately, and also for stereo you keep as stereo (2.0 \u2192 2.1, sent as 5.1).\n• Off.\n\nWithout a dedicated subwoofer LegacyAVR never generates an LFE channel (3.1 becomes 3.0, 5.1 becomes 5.0) and the switch decides what happens to an LFE that the source already has (5.1 / 7.1 films and games):\n• Fold into front L/R (recommended, default): the LFE is low-passed at 120 Hz and added to the front left and right speakers at -3 dB each, so its power is kept; the safety limiter prevents clipping. The LFE trim on the Mixer sets how loud it is.\n• Off: the source's LFE is discarded, and the LFE indicator says so.\nPCM 2.0 output has no LFE channel either, so the source's LFE is folded the same way before the stereo downmix.",
            "A source with its own LFE is never touched. Generated bass is derived from the mix, it is not a real effects track.\n• Native-LFE safeguard (v1.3.0): a 5.1/7.1 film can have a silent LFE for minutes and look like 5.0. As soon as the input's LFE channel (found from the cable's speaker layout, not from which speakers are currently active) carries real signal above -60 dBFS, Auto stops generating for the rest of that playback, fading out with the usual ramp. It only resumes after all input channels have been silent (below -80 dBFS) for the \"Resume after silence\" time.\n• While \"LFE generation while gaming\" is on and Game Mode is active, this control is greyed out because the game setting overrides it.",
            "Auto with a subwoofer; Fold into front L/R without one.",
            "lfe generation 5.0 5.1 .1 upmix subwoofer bass 7.0 games inhibit native lfe paused quiet fold discard no subwoofer fronts"),
        new("Stereo & upmix", "LFE generation: resume after silence",
            "After the source has used its own LFE channel, Auto (and Game Mode) LFE generation stays paused until every input channel has been digitally silent for this long. Silence means below -80 dBFS, not literally 0 dBFS. Any sound restarts the countdown. The pause also survives format changes and output re-opens.",
            "Whole seconds from 1 to 300. Shorter values resume generation sooner between films or episodes; longer values protect against quiet passages inside a film being treated as the end of playback. The same range is used by the engine, its configuration file, profiles and imports; values outside it are corrected (or refused by the engine's command interface).",
            "10 s - the default and the recommendation. Raise it to 30-60 s if generation comes back during a quiet scene of a 5.1 film.",
            "lfe reset silence interval resume inhibit pause seconds timeout"),
        new("Stereo & upmix", "Game mode",
            "Games often switch between stereo (menus, cut-scenes) and surround. Game mode stops LegacyAVR from reacting: stereo is not upmixed, \"Lossless PCM 2.0\" does not switch the output, and stereo is sent inside a 5.1 stream so the AVR never re-syncs. Real 5.1/7.1 game audio is passed as usual, and LFE generation still works (see \"LFE generation while gaming\" to force it on).\n• Auto-detect: on while a full-screen game is in the foreground (browsers, video players and other apps are ignored).\n• Always on / Off.",
            "Auto-detection runs in this dashboard: if the dashboard is closed, the engine leaves game mode after 6 s. Borderless-window games are detected when the window covers the whole monitor.",
            "Off (the default since 1.5.0 - an existing configuration keeps its setting). Choose Auto-detect if your games jump between stereo and surround.",
            "game gaming detection fullscreen churn stereo games no upmix pcm switch"),
        new("Stereo & upmix", "Game Mode: LFE generation while gaming",
            "A switch in the Game Mode card. When it is on and Game Mode is active, LFE generation is forced on (like \"Always\": 5.0 \u2192 5.1 and stereo \u2192 2.1) whatever the general LFE generation setting is, and the general control is greyed out. When Game Mode is not active, or the switch is off, the general setting works exactly as before.",
            "The native-LFE safeguard still applies: if the game sends its own LFE, generation pauses until the input has been silent for the \"Resume after silence\" time. Needs a dedicated subwoofer: without one nothing is generated and the switch is greyed out.",
            "On (the default since 1.5.0 - an existing configuration keeps its setting). It only acts while Game Mode is active.",
